<p>Ukrainian anti-corruption authorities have served Andriy Yermak, President Volodymyr Zelenskyy’s former chief of staff, with an official notice of suspicion in a money-laundering case tied to a luxury housing project near Kyiv, according to Ukrainian media and anti-graft agencies. NABU and SAPO said an organized group laundered 460 million hryvnias, or about $10.5 million, through elite construction; Ukrainian media identified the unnamed former presidential office head in the agencies’ statement as Yermak. The case is part of a wider corruption inquiry that previously focused on a reported $100 million kickback scheme linked to state nuclear power company Energoatom and people close to Zelenskyy, including businessman Timur Mindich and former senior official Oleksiy Chernyshov, who have denied allegations in the broader probe. Yermak left office in November 2025 after anti-corruption investigators searched him or his office as the scandal widened; Politico reported that Zelenskyy fired him and said he wanted to avoid speculation. Yermak denied owning property in the housing development and told journalists in Kyiv that he had no mansions, only a flat and a car.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/NVJDrF-kYnCd_EroqW5Om8zonFOHKuCRrEknFGvevrSr-aqdUnm2Q_Suc6vTDr9ANcxpoXPNwq5GUh974a7KtfZQ590WK_se6v17e0NmBj2ltyQchfL7uUgF2_r6MeQSHsHoFf0XFKXYCqMDMgtLFuIsHBIPvIvADn972cRPSJRrtwt7wrazGeXMLPGlXM6JgkaEv6kdB1qriblj-q1ZnLeryzTq0pi9' alt='Andriy Yermak resigned in November after law enforcement agencies searched his apartment as part of a corruption investigation, but he was not charged.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Legal exposure: Reports citing Ukrainian media said the alleged offense falls under Article 209 of Ukraine’s Criminal Code on money laundering. Some Czech and Slovak reports said a conviction could carry up to 12 years in prison and confiscation of property.</li><li>Housing project: RBC-Ukraine reported that investigators linked the allegations to private residences called “Dynastia,” while Montenegrin outlet Dan described the project as being in Kozyn near Kyiv.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>NABU and SAPO: The agencies said the latest charges relate to the so-called Midas operation, the name used for the wider anti-corruption probe. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/former-zelenskyy-chief-staff-charged-with-corruption-andriy-yermak/'>Politico</a>)</li><li>Andriy Yermak: Yermak said he would provide a fuller response after the investigation is complete. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/12/zelenskyy-ex-chief-of-staff-andriy-yermak-accused-ukraine-corruption-investigation'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://meduza.io/en/news/2026/05/11/ukraine-names-zelensky-s-former-chief-of-staff-andrii-yermak-a-suspect-in-money-laundering-case'>Ukraine names Zelensky’s former chief of staff, Andriy Yermak, as a suspect in a major money laundering case</a> - meduza.io</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/may/12/zelenskyy-ex-chief-of-staff-andriy-yermak-accused-ukraine-corruption-investigation'>Zelenskyy’s ex-chief of staff accused in Ukraine corruption investigation</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/yermak-zelenskyy-suspicion-ukraine-investigation/33754687.html'>Former Head Of Zelenskyy&#x27;s Office Under Suspicion In Major Ukrainian Investigation</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/11/world/europe/ukraine-corruption-yermak-zelensky.html'>Former Top Zelensky Aide Is Accused in Corruption Inquiry</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reddit.com/r/worldnews/comments/1tae3fa/zelenskys_former_secondincommand_yermak_charged/'>Zelensky&#x27;s former second-in-command, Yermak, charged in major corruption probe</a> - reddit.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/russia/639869-zelenskys-former-chief-of-staff-criminal/'>Former Zelensky chief of staff faces new criminal case</a> - rt.com